Objective:

Accept user input (Name, Email)

Check if email is provided

Send email notification if email exists

Log messages for tracking

Return response to client

🔷 2. Integration Flow Overview
🧩 Flow Structure:
Trigger → Switch
            ├── IF (Email exists)
            │       → Logger
            │       → Assign
            │       → Notification (Send Email)
            │       → Map (Response)
            │
            └── OTHERWISE (No Email)
                    → Logger
                    → Map (Response)
🔷 3. Components Explanation
🔹 3.1 Trigger (REST Adapter)

Entry point of the integration

Accepts input from user/API

📥 Sample Input:
{
  "name": "John",
  "email": "abc@gmail.com"
}
🔹 3.2 Switch (Decision Logic)

Used to apply conditional logic

✔ Condition:
If email is NOT empty → Route 1
Else → Otherwise
🔹 3.3 Route 1 (Email Exists)

This path executes when email is provided.

🪵 a) Logger

Logs runtime message

Helps in debugging

Example Message:

Email received, processing request
🪵 b) Assign

Stores values into variables

Optional step

Example Use:

Store name/email for later use

🪵 c) Notification (Send Email)

Sends email to provided email ID

Configuration:

To: Email from input

Subject: Hello from OIC

Body:

<html>
  <body>
    <p>Hello,</p>
    <p>Welcome to Oracle Integration Cloud.</p>
  </body>
</html>
🪵 d) Map (Response)

Prepares response sent back to client

Example Output:

{
  "message": "Email sent successfully"
}
🔹 3.4 Otherwise (No Email)

Executed when email is empty or missing.

🪵 a) Logger
Email not provided
🪵 b) Map (Response)
{
  "message": "No email provided"
}
🔷 4. Final Response

Both branches return response to client

Ensures API always sends output
